problems.problem:
Do there exist positive integers $k$, $m$, $n$ greater than $1$ such that a rectangular board $m\times n$ has the following two properties:
1) it can be cut (along grid lines) into $\frac{mn}{k}$ rectangles $1\times k$;
2) it is possible to cut out $\frac{mn}{k}-1$ rectangles $1\times k$ (along grid lines) so that the other $k$ squares form a figure that is not a rectangle, but every two squares in it can be connected by a rook path (this path goes through squares of this figure)?

Does there exist a function $f$ defined on the set of all pairs of real numbers that satisfies the conditions:
1) $f$ takes on all real numbers;
2) for any real $x$ and $y$: $f(x,y)=-f(y,x)$;
3) for any real numbers $x$, $y$, $z$: $f(x,f(y,z))=2001f(f(x,y),z)$?

Solve the system of equalities
$$\begin{cases}|x_1-x_2|=ax_3,\\ |x_2-x_3|=ax_4,\\ \quad\ldots\\ |x_{2007}-x_1|=ax_2\end{cases}$$
if the parameter $a$ satisfies the conditions:
a) $a>1$;
b) $a=1$.

It is permitted in one step to replace the triple $(a,b,c)$ by the triple $\left(\sqrt{2a^2+2b^2-c^2},\ \sqrt{2b^2+2c^2-a^2},\ \sqrt{2c^2+2a^2-b^2}\right)$. Is it possible to get after a finite number of steps from the triple $\left(3,\sqrt{19},\sqrt{22}\right)$:
a) the triple $\left(\sqrt{3^{2007}\cdot2},\ \sqrt{3^{2007}\cdot4},\ \sqrt{3^{2007}\cdot6}\right)$;
b) the triple $\left(\sqrt{3^{2008}\cdot4},\ \sqrt{3^{2008}\cdot13},\ \sqrt{3^{2008}\cdot33}\right)$?

Can the number 2016 be represented as a sum of cubes of

a) three natural numbers;

b) four natural numbers?

On a $6n\times 6n$ square, where $n$ is a natural number, Oleksii and Solomiia play the following game. With each move Oleksii places on the board a figure of type $F$ consisting of three cells. Solomiia in turn, after each move of Oleksii, places the numbers 0, 1 and 2 into the cells of the figure placed by Oleksii, using each of the numbers exactly once. If after Oleksii's next move two placed figures have a common cell, he immediately loses. If the square has become completely filled with numbers, the game stops. In this case, if the sum of the numbers in every row and every column is divisible by 3, Solomiia wins; otherwise Oleksii wins. Who can secure a win in this game if the figure of type $F$ is:

a) a $1\times 3$ rectangle;

b) a corner of three cells?
